INT. LUNAR ELEVATOR - CAR 4 - CONTINUOUS

A narrow steel cabin, ribbed with cable housing, one round window. Earth hangs beyond it, impossibly bright. Warning lights pulse amber.

CAPTAIN IRENE VOSS, 40s, in a pressure suit with the helmet clipped at her hip, braces against the wall with one hand, a tablet in the other. Her partner TAMSIN OKORO, 30s, in the same suit, hangs from the ceiling rail, trying to rewire a panel with a screwdriver in her teeth. A bolt snaps. The car lurches. Tamsin swings out, catches Irene by the shoulder, and both women slam against the window. The tablet floats loose. Irene grabs it. Tamsin spits out the screwdriver and clamps it to her chest. The lights go red.

IRENE
Status!

TAMSIN
Cable two's gone. We're on three and four.

IRENE
Can they hold us?

TAMSIN
For nine minutes.

IRENE
(to herself)
Nine minutes.

TAMSIN
Eight and a half.

IRENE
Everyone in the cabin, seat belts!

TAMSIN
There's only you and me.

IRENE
I know. It's a figure of speech.

A thunk. A groan from the housing. Then a soft chime. The lights return to amber. The car steadies.

They stare at each other. Tamsin starts to laugh. Irene joins her. They drift apart in the microgravity, still laughing, still holding hands.
